WHAT IS DEPLOYMENT STRATEGY
These are the techniques which are used to manage the rollout and scaling of applications within a Kubernetes cluster.
Types of Deployment Strategies
  1) Canary Deployment
  2) Recreate Deployment
  3) Rolling Update
  4) Blue-Green Deployment
1. CANARY DEPLOYMENT
A Canary Deployment involves rolling out a new version of your application to a subset of your pods or a 
percentage of your traffic to test it before deploying it to the entire application in production.
2. RECREATE DEPLOYMENT:
In this strategy, the existing version of the application is terminated completely, and a new version is 
deployed in its place. This approach is simple but may cause downtime during the update.
  -> we use it pre production not in production
3. Rolling Update   ( Its default deployment strategy in K8's)
  Create new pods
    It starts by creating a few pods of the new version.
  Monitor Pods
    It monitors the new pods to make sure they are healthy and working well.
  Route traffic
    If the new pods are working fine, Kubernetes gradually increases their number while reducing the old pods.
  Delete old pods
    This process continues until all pods are running the new version, and the old version has been phased out.


Blue-Green Deployment is a deployment strategy used in Kubernetes (K8s) and other platforms to release new application versions with zero or minimal downtime and easy rollback.

Concept

You maintain two identical environments:

Blue = Current production version (live traffic)
Green = New version you want to release

Example:

Users
  |
  v
Service ---> Blue Pods (v1)  <-- Live
             Green Pods (v2) <-- Not receiving traffic

After testing Green:

Users
  |
  v
Service ---> Green Pods (v2) <-- Live
             Blue Pods (v1)  <-- Idle

The switch happens by changing the Kubernetes Service selector or updating the load balancer routing.

-------> JOBS <-----------
KUBERNETES JOBS:
   It is a resource that is used to achieve a particular work like backup script and, once the work is completed the pod will be deleted.
  Use cases:
    Database backup script needs to run
    Running batch processes
    Running the task on the scheduled interval
    Log Rotation
  Key Features:
    One-time Execution: If you have a task that needs to be executed one time whether it's succeed or fail then the job will be finished.
    Parallelism: If you want to run multiple pods at the same time.
    Scheduling: If you want to schedule a specific number of pods after a specific time.
    Restart Policy: You can specify whether the Job should restart if fails.

